    "rich_text_AHWmYY": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "richtext_Kt6Cwh": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>TIP: These are recommendations rather than rules. </p><p>The most important question is not simply which finger should a promise ring go on? It is: what do you want your promise ring to say?</p><p><em>“Your promise doesn't need a rulebook. There are traditions worth knowing, but the right finger is ultimately the one that fits what your ring means to you.”</em></p><p>- The Promise Co. Team</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "richtext_Kt6Cwh"
      ],
      "name": "t:sections.rich_text.presets.rich_text.name",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "scheme-1",
        "separate_section_with_border": true,
        "content_width": "sm",
        "text_position": "center",
        "remove_vertical_spacing": false,
        "use_baka_too_font": false,
        "use_ogg_font": false,
        "use_compact_spacing": false,
        "section_id": ""
      }
    },
    "image_with_text_TdcT6e": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "subheading_ENzxWq": {
          "type": "subheading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "promise rings"
          }
        },
        "heading_QfEdY6": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Should a Promise Ring Go on Your Left or Right Hand?",
            "heading_tag": "h1"
          }
        },
        "richtext_CAEibM": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>Both are completely acceptable. The difference is largely about how other people are likely to interpret the ring.</p><p>In mainstream US culture, the fourth finger of the left hand is strongly associated with engagement and marriage. That means wearing a promise ring there can make it look more like part of the traditional progression toward engagement. The right hand creates more separation.</p><p><em>TIP: There's no universal rule. Choose the finger that fits what the promise actually means.</em></p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "subheading_ENzxWq",
        "heading_QfEdY6",
        "richtext_CAEibM"
      ],
      "name": "t:sections.image_with_text.presets.image_with_text.name",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "scheme-2",
        "separate_section_with_border": true,
        "image": "shopify://shop_images/bannerdesk2.heic",
        "reveal_on_scroll": true,
        "image_position": "end",
        "content_width": "sm",
        "remove_vertical_spacing": true
      }
    },
    "rich_text_9HgCnJ": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "subheading_G7xmRp": {
          "type": "subheading",
          "settings": {
            "text": ""
          }
        },
        "heading_A9x8Cb": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Promise ring on the left hand",
            "heading_tag": "h1"
          }
        },
        "richtext_3N7JLc": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>The left ring finger is one of the most traditional places to wear a romantic promise ring. It works particularly well if the meaning behind the ring is:</p><p>For some couples, the promise ring effectively holds the place that an engagement ring may occupy later. Once an engagement happens, the promise ring might then be moved to the right hand, another finger, added to a ring stack, or even worn on a necklace.</p><p>The main downside is obvious: other people may assume you are already engaged. The more engagement-like the ring looks, the more likely that becomes. A diamond solitaire worn on the left ring finger, for example, may be interpreted very differently from a small engraved band worn in exactly the same place.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "subheading_G7xmRp",
        "heading_A9x8Cb",
        "richtext_3N7JLc"
      ],
      "name": "t:sections.rich_text.presets.rich_text.name",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "scheme-1",
        "separate_section_with_border": true,
        "content_width": "sm",
        "text_position": "start",
        "remove_vertical_spacing": false,
        "use_baka_too_font": false,
        "use_ogg_font": false,
        "use_compact_spacing": false,
        "section_id": ""
      }
    },
    "rich_text_G6NGN9": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_FVhy8A": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Promise ring on the right hand",
            "heading_tag": "h1"
          }
        },
        "richtext_gNeqy9": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>For many couples, the right ring finger is the best all-around promise-ring placement. It still uses a finger naturally associated with meaningful rings while avoiding the finger Americans most strongly associate with engagement.</p><p>It can also make things simpler later. If you eventually receive an engagement ring, the promise ring can stay exactly where it is rather than needing a new home.</p><p>There are cultural exceptions. Some cultures and religious traditions use the right hand for wedding rings, meaning a right-hand ring is not universally non-marital. But for most<br/>mainstream US wearers, the right ring finger creates considerably less engagement<br/>confusion than the left.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "heading_FVhy8A",
        "richtext_gNeqy9"
      ],
      "name": "t:sections.rich_text.presets.rich_text.name",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "scheme-2",
        "separate_section_with_border": true,
        "content_width": "sm",
        "text_position": "end",
        "remove_vertical_spacing": false,
        "use_baka_too_font": false,
        "use_ogg_font": false,
        "use_compact_spacing": false,
        "section_id": ""
      }
    },
    "image_with_text_overlay_Rhtg7n": {
      "type": "image-with-text-overlay",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_AXqWXG": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "So which is better?",
            "heading_tag": "h4"
          }
        },
        "richtext_VKik3h": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>Choose the left ring finger if the promise is deliberately connected with a future engagement. Choose the right ring finger if the promise represents romantic commitment but you do not want it to look like an engagement ring. Neither choice makes the promise more or less meaningful.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "heading_AXqWXG",
        "richtext_VKik3h"
      ],
