    "faq_6rXbCj": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "question_kCpKca": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Does the ring mean “we plan to get engaged”?",
            "answer": "<p>If yes, the left ring finger is probably the strongest choice. It makes the promise feel connected to the traditional progression from dating to engagement to marriage.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_3NEHUa": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do you want other people to recognize it as romantic?",
            "answer": "<p>If yes, choose the right ring finger. It still reads naturally as important relationship jewelry without suggesting quite as strongly that you are engaged. If public interpretation<br/>does not matter, a middle or index finger works equally well.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_TbyEE3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Are you expecting an engagement ring soon?",
            "answer": "<p>Think about where you eventually want both rings to live. If you do not want to move the promise ring later, avoid the left ring finger now. The right ring finger or middle finger gives the promise ring a permanent home while leaving room for a future engagement ring.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_e7NCgr": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What does the ring actually look like?",
            "answer": "<p>Placement and design work together. A small engraved band works almost anywhere. A large solitaire worn on the left ring finger may easily be mistaken for an engagement ring. A wide sculptural band may look better and feel more comfortable on a middle or index finger. A small personalized ring might suit the pinky perfectly.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_9cNJbj": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Which finger is most comfortable?",
            "answer": "<p>Symbolism matters far less if you do not actually enjoy wearing your ring. Your hands are not perfectly symmetrical either. A ring that fits the ring finger on your right hand will not necessarily fit the corresponding finger on your left. Measure the exact finger and hand where you plan to wear the ring.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "question_kCpKca",
        "question_3NEHUa",
        "question_TbyEE3",
        "question_e7NCgr",
        "question_9cNJbj"
      ],
      "name": "t:sections.faq.presets.faq.name",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "scheme-b68b61c5-ba0e-4161-a0e3-3d59622e9bf4",
        "separate_section_with_border": true,
        "anchor_id": "",
        "show_categories": false,
        "subheading": "if you're still unsure, work through these questions:",
        "title": "How to Choose the Right Finger for Your Promise Ring",
        "content": ""
      }
    },
    "rich_text_9YCJBh": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_PzGbFx": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Left ring finger",
            "heading_tag": "h1"
          }
        },
        "richtext_iWfw9G": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>Best for: pre-engagement promises. The left ring finger carries the strongest relationship-status symbolism in American culture. It is traditionally where an engagement ring is worn, followed by or combined with a wedding band. That makes it particularly powerful for a promise ring when the promise is connected to eventual marriage.</p><p>Advantages include an immediately romantic feel, a clear signal that the relationship is serious, and a natural progression toward an engagement ring.The main consideration is potential confusion: people may see a ring on your left ring finger and assume you are engaged, particularly if the ring features a prominent center stone.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_QW4iT6": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Right ring finger",
            "heading_tag": "h1"
          }
        },
        "richtext_Vi9i4J": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>Best for: serious romantic commitment without engagement. The right ring finger gives you much of the emotional symbolism of the left ring finger without occupying America's conventional engagement-ring position. For that reason, it is arguably the most versatile promise-ring finger.</p><p>It clearly feels like a meaningful ring, has a lower risk of being mistaken for an engagement ring, can remain in place if you later become engaged, and works well for both understated and more elaborate designs.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_XpKCBt": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Left middle finger",
            "heading_tag": "h1"
          }
        },
        "richtext_TLeHbN": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>Best for: modern romantic promises or self-promise rings. The middle finger gives a promise ring plenty of visibility while keeping the traditional engagement finger available. GIA includes the left middle finger among popular promise-ring positions.</p><p>It is highly visible, clearly different from an engagement ring, and works particularly well with simple bands and contemporary designs. If you already wear rings on your ring or index fingers, however, a wider promise ring may make the hand feel crowded.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_fkPjhg": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Right middle finger",
            "heading_tag": "h1"
          }
        },
        "richtext_JLRTCi": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>Best for: commitment with minimal relationship-status ambiguity. The right middle finger is one of the most neutral yet prominent options available. It works particularly well for couples who want their ring to be personally meaningful without expecting strangers to understand its relationship significance.</p><p>It is also one of the strongest choices for friendship and self-promise rings. The trade-off is that other people may simply see it as jewelry - which, for many wearers, is not a disadvantage at all.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_z3cmdJ": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Left index finger",
            "heading_tag": "h1"
          }
        },
        "richtext_ztBiWK": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>Best for: statement designs and contemporary promise rings. An index-finger promise ring immediately looks less traditional. It can be an especially good position for sculptural, wide, signet-style, or distinctive rings.</p><p>It is very visible, creates minimal engagement confusion, and clearly separates the ring from bridal traditions. Its romantic meaning, however, may not be obvious to other people.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_kfGWbn": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Thumb",
            "heading_tag": "h1"
          }
        },
        "richtext_8TFnJA": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>Best for: highly personal or unconventional promise rings. Neither thumb has a standardized promise-ring meaning in the United States. That makes  a thumb ring a very personal choice.</p><p>If you are wearing a ring to represent a promise to yourself or deliberately want to avoid traditional relationship symbolism, the thumb can be appealing. Try the ring during normal daily tasks before deciding it is comfortable enough for permanent wear, because the thumb is heavily involved in gripping and handling objects.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_WYBkJd": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Pinky finger",
            "heading_tag": "h1"
          }
        },
        "richtext_7PcL7y": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>Best for: discreet, personalized, or signet-inspired designs. Promise rings can also be worn on either pinky. This placement is considerably less likely to be interpreted as romantic relationship jewelry.</p><p>That makes it suitable for friendship promises, self-promises, family commitments, signet-style rings, and small personalized bands. Larger gemstone designs may be less practical because the pinky provides less space and support than larger fingers.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
