    "rich_text_3iUw7W": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_ApnTaG": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Promise Ring Style and Finger Placement",
            "heading_tag": "h1"
          }
        },
        "richtext_KJUMta": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>The design of the promise ring can change the way its placement is interpreted.</p><p>A solitaire stone has particularly strong engagement-ring associations. Wear one on the left ring finger and many people are likely to assume it is an engagement ring. If that is not the message you want to send, the right hand or another finger makes the distinction much clearer.</p><p>Heart-shaped motifs naturally communicate affection without looking quite as bridal as a solitaire. They work especially well on either ring finger or the middle finger.</p><p>Infinity and knot designs are popular because their symbolism naturally relates to connection and continuity. They can work on virtually any finger and are particularly suitable when the promise represents lasting commitment rather than a future proposal.</p><p>Birthstones make the promise more personal and can help differentiate a ring from traditional engagement jewelry. They work especially well for romantic, friendship, family, and self-promise rings.</p><p>A simple metal band can be extremely meaningful while remaining understated. On the left ring finger, however, it can potentially resemble a wedding band. If avoiding marriage-related assumptions matters, the right hand or middle finger may be preferable.</p><p>Contemporary, chunky, or sculptural designs often look especially natural on the middle or index finger. The extra space can also improve comfort.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "heading_ApnTaG",
        "richtext_KJUMta"
      ],
      "name": "t:sections.rich_text.presets.rich_text.name",
