    "rich_text_gQCrHn": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "subheading_MFAzJ7": {
          "type": "subheading",
          "settings": {
            "text": ""
          }
        },
        "heading_wcbbM3": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Traditional vs. Modern Promise Ring Practice",
            "heading_tag": "h1"
          }
        },
        "richtext_V7G3yd": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>The idea of using rings to symbolize promises is much older than the modern term “promise ring.” Historical predecessors include medieval posy rings, which were engraved with messages of affection, and Georgian and Victorian sentimental rings that sometimes used gemstones to encode words or personal meanings.</p><p>But that history does not create a direct rule saying today's promise ring must be worn on one particular finger. Modern promise-ring practice is considerably more flexible.</p><p>•   A path toward engagement</p><p>•   A committed relationship without marriage</p><p>•   Mutual commitment between two partners</p><p>•   Friendship</p><p>•   Personal promises</p><p>•   Long-distance relationships</p><p>•   Important milestones</p><p>That broader meaning is one reason modern finger placement is much less standardized than engagement and wedding-ring placement.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "subheading_MFAzJ7",
        "heading_wcbbM3",
        "richtext_V7G3yd"
      ],
