    "rich_text_D3Diwr": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_qmLVPJ": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Are There Different Promise Ring Traditions Around the US?",
            "heading_tag": "h1"
          }
        },
        "richtext_QHg7JB": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>There does not appear to be an established promise-ring convention dividing the United States by region. There is not a recognized rule saying people in the South wear promise rings on one hand while people in the Northeast, Midwest, or West use another.</p><p>Individual families, communities, and cultural traditions can absolutely interpret rings  differently, but cultural heritage is much more relevant than US geography.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_iGT76X": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Claddagh rings",
            "heading_tag": "h1"
          }
        },
        "richtext_MQ9dkA": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>The Irish Claddagh ring is an excellent example of a ring that already has its own wearing traditions. Its heart, hands, and crown represent love, friendship, and loyalty. Traditional interpretations can vary based on which hand wears the ring and whether the heart faces inward or outward.</p><p>Someone wearing a Claddagh as a promise ring may therefore want to follow its own cultural conventions rather than generic promise-ring advice.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_PHRefH": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Jewish wedding traditions",
            "heading_tag": "h1"
          }
        },
        "richtext_YzJCkY": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>Traditional Jewish wedding ceremonies can involve placing a wedding ring on the bride's right index finger during the ceremony. This is not a promise-ring rule. It simply demonstrates why ring symbolism is not identical across every cultural background in the United States.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_TNn9kC": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Orthodox Christian traditions",
            "heading_tag": "h1"
          }
        },
        "richtext_zXVYCW": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>Wedding rings are traditionally worn on the right hand in several Orthodox Christian cultures. For someone from that background, the mainstream American idea that “right hand means not married” may not apply in quite the same way.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_zjeLxi": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Korean couple rings",
            "heading_tag": "h1"
          }
        },
        "richtext_T7nY8y": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>In South Korea, couples may exchange matching or coordinating “couple rings” to symbolize their relationship. They are sometimes associated with relationship milestones such as the 100-day anniversary. </p><p>For Korean-American couples familiar with that tradition, the symbolism of exchanging rings may differ from the conventional American idea of a promise ring as something that sits somewhere between dating and engagement.</p><p>If your culture, religion, family, or ring design already gives a particular hand or finger a meaning,<br/>that tradition can matter more than generic promise-ring etiquette.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
